-- Close the PostgREST grant hole on the public schema (2026-09-16). Generated by
-- scripts/audit_grants.py --emit; one plain statement per object, nothing dynamic.
--
-- Supabase's default ACL grants every object postgres creates in public to anon and
-- authenticated. Tables were saved by RLS; VIEWS run with their owner's rights and were
-- readable with the publishable key (v_sales with customer names, v_catalog_cost, stock,
-- margins). Three tables had no RLS and were writable. See the header of audit_grants.py
-- for why revoking is safe. Rollback = GRANT the same privileges back (do not).
--
-- Apply:  python -m scripts.apply_sql scripts/hotfix_view_grants_migration.sql
-- Verify: python -m scripts.audit_grants   (exit 0 = nothing exposed)

-- 1. Row-level security on every table (idempotent; service role bypasses RLS). Where the
--    assistant's yq_readonly role holds a SELECT grant, keep its read via a policy, mirroring
--    the existing *_yq_readonly_read pattern from scripts/security_migration.sql.
